  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nMid-level ridging will continue to promote widespread subsidence and \nhot, mainly dry conditions through Wednesday night/early \nThursday. Rain chances are expected to remain minimal, with highs \nclimbing into the 90s along the coast, with triple digits \nelsewhere. Low- level moisture will remain sufficient enough for \npeak heat index values to reach up to 110-113 degF, resulting in a\ncontinued moderate to major (levels 2/3- 4) HeatRisk. As a \nresult, a Heat Advisory will be in effect on Wednesday throughout \nmuch of the CWA. With similar conditions expected on Thursday, an \nadditional Heat Advisory will likely be needed.\n\nWe are continuing to monitor Tropical Storm Bertha across the \nnorthern Gulf. Bertha has strengthened some, but increasing \nnortheasterly vertical wind shear has displaced the deepest \nconvection to the south of the low-level center. While slight \nadditional strengthening is possible through today, the combination \nof shear, dry air entrainment, and eventual land interaction along \nthe Louisiana Coast is expected to promote gradual weakening by late \nweek.\n\nThe latest guidance continues to keep the center north of us, \nwith the most significant impacts expected from southeast \nLouisiana eastward. However, the westward extent of the storm's \ntropical moisture is causing some uncertainty. Given this, low \nrain chances have been maintained along the Coastal Bend late this\nweek to account for any outer rain bands. At this time, any \nrainfall is expected to remain isolated to scattered with minimal \naccumulations. Even with Bertha likely remaining to our north, we \nwill still see impacts in the way of increasing easterly swell, an\nelevated risk of rip currents, and higher surf this weekend. \n\nBy early next week, we are looking at dry conditions returning and \nabove-normal temperatures persisting as ridging reestablishes itself \nacross the south-central CONUS. High temperatures will generally be \nin the 90s along the coast with triple digits inland.",
